data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"file_path\": \"/home/<USER:1>/.local/share/cs-campaign/output/mission-report.md\", \"content\": \"# Mission report: greet, tested\\n\\n## Outcome: met\\n\\nAll three stated criteria are satisfied, verified independently (not just from\\ndev's report):\\n\\n1. `src/greet.py` defines `greet(name)` returning `f\\\"Hello, {name}!\\\"`.\\n2. `tests/test_greet.py` covers a normal name (`greet(\\\"World\\\") == \\\"Hello, World!\\\"`)\\n   and the empty string (`greet(\\\"\\\") == \\\"Hello, !\\\"`).\\n3. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` exits 0 (2 tests, OK) — confirmed by\\n   checking out dev's branch (`refs/remotes/campaign/dev/subject`, commit\\n   `e0f0487`) into a scratch worktree and running the suite myself.\\n\\n## How it was done\\n\\n- Dispatched the full spec to `dev` in one round (d002).\\n- dev implemented both files, ran the suite, and committed to its own branch\\n  (`cs-sandbox/<DEV:1>.<GROUP:1>`) on top of the shared base\\n  (`852c73f`).\\n- Judged by fetching `refs/remotes/campaign/dev/subject`, inspecting the diff\\n  and file contents directly, and re-running the test command myself in an\\n  isolated worktree rather than trusting the reported output. Result matched\\n  dev's claim exactly (exit 0, 2 tests passed).\\n- Accepted on first attempt — no rework was needed.\\n\\n## Orchestrator branch\\n\\nNo source changes were made by the orchestrator (per role, orchestrator writes\\nno code); orchestrator's own branch carries no repo changes, which is expected\\nfor this mission.\\n\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":0,"type":"content_block_delta"}

